+# Privacy implementation and third-party audit
+
+Reviewed on October 23, 2026. Scope: every shipped HTML/CSS file, runtime
+dependencies, browser storage, permissions, and deployment headers. This was a
+source audit; no browser, dev server, or live deployment was used.
+
+## Data inventory
+
+| Surface | Finding and handling |
+| --- | --- |
+| Informational pages | No forms or personal information requested. |
+| Client scripts | None needed or shipped; CSP disables all scripts, workers, network APIs, and frames. No analytics, fingerprinting, cookies, storage, or device permission requests. |
+| Hero photograph | Previously fetched from `images.unsplash.com`, exposing visitor IP and request metadata. Now served locally as `assets/coffee.jpg`; no visitor request goes to Unsplash. Source: https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085 . Retain the image's applicable license when redistributing. |
+| Fonts and CSS | System fonts and local stylesheet; no font/CDN requests. |
+| Runtime dependencies | None bundled. The existing `statikk` start command is development tooling, not a third-party browser integration. |
+| Hosting | The selected host necessarily processes IP addresses and request metadata. Logging, access controls, retention, and deletion remain host configuration responsibilities; no hosting account was available for audit. |
+| Accounts, orders, logout | Existing navigation targets have no backend in this repository. The misleading GET logout link was replaced with a working privacy link. No session termination or data deletion is claimed. Other existing navigation destinations are unchanged. |
+
+## Deployment
+
+Run `npm run build`. It copies only public files into `dist/` and audits those
+files for third-party references, active content, CSP drift, missing local
+assets, and privacy/security header regressions. This check does not inspect
+runtime traffic or verify a hosting provider's behavior.
+
+`netlify.toml` publishes `dist/`; its `_headers` supplies real response headers
+on Netlify. Cloudflare Pages also supports the emitted `_headers` format: set
+the build command to `npm run build` and output directory to `dist`. Other hosts
+must translate `_headers` into their own response-header configuration. Do not
+publish the repository root. The existing `npm start` utility is not configured
+to enforce these response headers.
+
+Both HTML documents carry a CSP and `no-referrer` meta fallback. HTTP-only
+protections (including `frame-ancestors`, Permissions-Policy, HSTS, and nosniff)
+require a host that honors the response configuration; meta tags cannot replace
+them. Unknown Permissions-Policy/Trusted Types directives are ignored by older
+browsers; script blocking and the absence of collection still apply.
+
+Serve exclusively over HTTPS and redirect HTTP at the host. HSTS starts at 300
+seconds; raise it after confirming HTTPS for the deployed domain. Subdomain
+coverage and preload are intentionally not enabled without a domain audit.
+The static site has no reporting backend: no CSP reporting collector or script
+samples are enabled, avoiding another channel for URLs or personal data.
+
+## Hosting and future data handling
+
+- Disable optional host analytics and unnecessary access logs. If security logs
+  are needed, document their purpose and shortest necessary retention period,
+  restrict access, mask IP addresses where feasible, and omit query strings,
+  credentials, tokens, and personal information. Configure expiry/deletion at
+  the host and update the public notice with the actual practices.
+- Before adding any collection, document each field's purpose, recipient,
+  retention, deletion process, and access/correction controls. Prefer guest
+  ordering; do not request precise location, birth dates, or account creation
+  unless essential. Separate any optional marketing choice from service access.
+- Keep sensitive data and credentials out of URLs, logs, browser storage, and
+  client bundles. Validate and authorize on the backend, encrypt transport and
+  storage, limit access, and rate-limit sensitive endpoints.
+- If accounts are implemented, use server-managed `__Host-` session cookies with
+  `Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/` and no `Domain`. Use a CSRF-protected
+  POST logout, revoke the server session, expire its cookie, and send
+  `Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache"` on successful logout only.
+  A static link or JavaScript storage clearing cannot revoke a session.
+- Do not add trackers merely to display a consent banner. New optional services
+  must stay unloaded until an informed choice; keep refusal and withdrawal easy.
+
+## Repeatable review
+
+Run the build audit on every change and review third parties before integration
+and at each release. Inventory origins, request fields, cookies/storage,
+recipients/subprocessors, retention, and deletion guarantees; remove anything
+unnecessary. Keep the enforced CSP restrictive. Version and integrity-pin any
+unavoidable external executable dependencies, and prefer locally hosted assets
+and click-to-load facades for embeds. Before a production integration, separately
+audit deployed response headers, host-injected analytics, and network requests;
+this source audit cannot rule out additions by the hosting platform.
